   "overlays": [],
   "project": "fieldnotes",
   "reply": "I added `fieldnotes delete INDEX`. `python tools/check.py` now passes, covering all 6 unit tests and the 100-character line-length rule.\n\n**Red step.** I wrote the tests first and ran them before any implementation. 4 of the 6 failed:\n- `store.delete` didn't exist, which gave an `AttributeError` in both store tests.\n- The CLI returned exit code 2 instead of 0 or 1 for `delete`.\n\n**What I changed**\n- **`fieldnotes/store.py`:**\n  - Added `delete(index)`, which removes and returns the note at that position. It raises `IndexError` for an out-of-range index and leaves the notes file unchanged in that case.\n  - Moved the file write into a small `_save` helper that `add` and `delete` share.\n- **`fieldnotes/cli.py`:**\n  - `delete N` prints `deleted: <text>` and exits 0.\n  - An out-of-range index prints `no note at index N` to stderr and exits 1.\n  - A non-numeric or missing argument prints the usage message and exits 2.\n  - I pulled the usage string into one `USAGE` constant that now mentions `delete`.\n- **Tests:** two new cases in `tests/test_store.py` and a new `tests/test_cli.py` with three cases.\n\n**Decision for you:** I made indexes 1-based, but `list` doesn't print numbers, so there's currently no way to see which index a note has. Numbering the `list` output would fix that, but it changes existing behaviour, so I left it alone. Say if you want it.\n\nI ran the checks with `NOTES_HOME` pointing at a temp directory, since `check.py` requires that variable.",
